spec:
  grid: 3 rows; width 9-11 cols; background black (0)
  sprites:
    count: 3-4 8-connected foreground components, ordered left-to-right with exactly 1 empty column gap between consecutive bboxes
    colors: each sprite has exactly 1 non-grey non-bg color (may repeat across sprites) plus grey (5) control pixels
    size: 1-3 rows x 2-4 cols; total sprite widths 7-9
    grey_markers: first sprite has 1 grey (right edge only); last sprite has 1 grey (left edge only); middle sprites have 2 grey (one on each edge); each edge marker is a single pixel
    seam_rule: exactly 1 grey pixel on rightmost column of sprite[k] and 1 on leftmost column of sprite[k+1]; row indices may differ (this controls vertical alignment)
    colored_cells: 1-4 per sprite; bg cells 0-3 within bbox
  layout: first sprite bbox starts at col 0 [observed]; sprites span most of the grid width
  output:
    height: 3 (same); width = sum of sprite bbox widths
    rule: crop each sprite to bbox, replace grey with sprite's color, concatenate left-to-right; first sprite keeps original top row, each subsequent sprite shifts vertically so seam markers align on same row
    constraint: all sprites must fit within 3 output rows after vertical alignment

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose N=3-4 sprites. For each, pick a distinct or shared non-grey color and a bbox size (1-3 rows x 2-4 cols, total width 7-9).
    2. Build the output by filling each sprite bbox with colored + bg cells (random connected shape, 1-4 colored cells).
    3. Assign vertical offsets (top row 0-2) for each sprite so it fits within 3 rows. Concatenate to form the output grid.
    4. To build input, replace one pixel on each seam with grey: for sprite[k], pick one colored cell on its rightmost column; for sprite[k+1], pick one colored cell on its leftmost column. These two grey pixels define the seam.
    5. Also add grey to the first sprite's right edge and last sprite's left edge (just one each, on a colored cell).
    6. Scatter the sprites back to the input grid: place left-to-right with exactly 1 empty column gap between consecutive bboxes, starting at col 0. Undo vertical alignment offsets so seam grey rows differ as needed.
    7. Verify: 8-connected components match N sprites, each has exactly 1 non-grey color, seam markers are correct, and assembled output fits in 3 rows.
